/* Geodesic — competition physics lessons. Generated by src/pcomp/build.py: every numerical answer was computed from the data in the question. */
WL.pcL({"id": "est", "area": "to", "title": "Estimation and dimensional analysis", "hook": ["The period T of a pendulum might depend on its length L, the mass m of the bob and g. Without solving any equation, what happens to T if the string is made four times longer?", "The units decide it. T is in seconds, L in metres, g in m s⁻² and m in kg. The only combination of L and g with units of seconds is √(L/g), and nothing else carries kilograms, so the mass cannot appear: T = C√(L/g), where C is a pure number (it turns out to be 2π for small swings). Four times the length doubles the period."], "ideas": [["Units are a constraint", "Write every quantity in terms of mass M, length L and time T. Both sides of a true equation have the same units, so if a quantity depends on a few others, matching the powers of M, L and T often fixes the formula up to a pure number. Deep-water waves of wavelength λ, driven by gravity: the speed can only be **v = C√(gλ)**."], ["When the units run out", "With more quantities than independent units, you get dimensionless groups and an unknown function of them. The range of a projectile depends on v, g and θ: units give R = (v²/g)·f(θ), but only physics finds f(θ) = sin 2θ. Angles, ratios and the arguments of sin, exp and log are always dimensionless."], ["Limiting cases check every answer", "Before trusting a formula, push a parameter to zero, to infinity, or make two masses equal. For an Atwood machine a = g(m₁ − m₂)/(m₁ + m₂): equal masses give 0, and m₂ = 0 gives free fall. A formula that fails one such test is wrong, however neat it looks."], ["Estimate in powers of ten", "A Fermi estimate keeps one significant figure and multiplies powers of ten. Air in a room 5 m × 4 m × 3 m: 60 m³ × 1.2 kg m⁻³ ≈ 70 kg. Write the chain of factors down, so you can see which one dominates the error."]], "ex": [{"q": "A liquid drop of radius r and density ρ is held in shape by surface tension γ (units N m⁻¹). Use units to find how the frequency f of its oscillations depends on r, ρ and γ.", "sol": ["[γ] = kg s⁻², [ρ] = kg m⁻³, [r] = m, and f is in s⁻¹. Try f = C γᵃ ρᵇ rᶜ.", "Kilograms: a + b = 0. Seconds: −2a = −1, so a = ½ and b = −½. Metres: −3b + c = 0, so c = −3/2.", "So f = C√(γ/(ρr³)). A drop twice as big oscillates 2^{3/2} ≈ 2.8 times more slowly."], "a": "f ∝ √(γ/(ρr³))"}, {"q": "Estimate the number of heartbeats in a human lifetime.", "sol": ["About 70 beats a minute, so about 10² per minute to one figure, or 7 × 10¹ if you want two.", "Minutes in 80 years: 80 × 365 × 24 × 60 ≈ 4 × 10⁷.", "Multiply: 7 × 10¹ × 4 × 10⁷ ≈ 3 × 10⁹ beats."], "a": "About 3 × 10⁹."}], "pr": [{"t": "mc", "lv": 1, "q": "The speed of a tsunami in water of depth d depends only on g and d. By what factor does its speed fall as it moves from water 4000 m deep to water 10 m deep?", "ch": ["4", "20", "400", "√20", "2"], "a": 1, "hint": "Which combination of g and d has units of speed?", "sol": "Units force v = C√(gd), so the speed scales as √d: √(4000/10) = √400 = 20. (That slowing is why a tsunami piles up near the shore.)"}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A satellite skims the surface of a spherical planet of uniform density 5500 kg m⁻³. Its period depends only on the density and G = 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻². Find the period in minutes.", "a": 84.452, "u": "min", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Write M in terms of the density and the radius, then use Kepler’s third law.", "sol": "Units alone give T = C/√(Gρ). Kepler: T² = 4π²R³/(GM) with M = (4/3)πR³ρ, so T² = 3π/(Gρ) and the radius cancels. T = √(3π/(6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ × 5500)) = 5067 s = 84.5 minutes, whatever the size of the planet."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A raindrop of radius 1.0 mm falls at its terminal speed. Model the drag as ½ρCAv² with C = 0.50, air density 1.2 kg m⁻³ and A the cross-section of the drop. Water has density 1000 kg m⁻³; g = 9.81 m s⁻². Find the terminal speed.", "a": 6.60303, "u": "m/s", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "At terminal speed the drag equals the weight.", "sol": "mg = ½ρCAv², with m = (4/3)πr³ρ_w = 4.19 × 10⁻⁶ kg and A = πr² = 3.14 × 10⁻⁶ m². So v = √(2mg/(ρCA)) = 6.60 m s⁻¹, close to the measured 6.5 m s⁻¹."}, {"t": "mc", "lv": 3, "q": "A block of mass m slides on a frictionless wedge of mass M and angle θ, and the wedge can slide on a frictionless floor. Which formula for the wedge’s acceleration passes all these checks: zero when θ = 0, zero when θ = 90°, zero as M → ∞, and the right units?", "ch": ["mg sin θ cos θ / (M + m sin²θ)", "mg sin θ / (M + m)", "mg cos θ / (M + m sin²θ)", "Mg sin θ cos θ / (M + m)", "mg tan θ / M"], "a": 0, "hint": "Test each option at θ = 0, at θ = 90° and with a very heavy wedge.", "sol": "B gives mg/(M + m) at θ = 90°, not zero. C gives mg/M at θ = 0. D tends to g sin θ cos θ, not zero, as M grows. E blows up at 90°. Only A survives every test, and it is the correct result."}], "traps": ["Units cannot find pure numbers such as 2π or ½, and cannot tell sin θ from θ.", "An exponent or the argument of a sine with units means the formula is wrong.", "An estimate needs one significant figure, not three: spend the time on the right physics."]});
WL.pcL({"id": "kin", "area": "me", "title": "Projectiles and relative motion", "hook": ["A monkey hangs from a branch. A hunter aims a dart straight at it, and the monkey lets go at the instant the dart is fired. Does the dart hit?", "Yes, as long as the dart reaches the monkey’s line before the ground. Both fall the same distance ½gt² below where they would have been without gravity, so a dart aimed along the straight line stays aimed at the monkey. In a frame falling with gravity, both move in straight lines."], "ideas": [["Split the motion", "Horizontal and vertical motion are independent: horizontally the velocity is constant, vertically the acceleration is −g. The time links them. Eliminating it gives the parabola y = x tan θ − gx²/(2v² cos²θ)."], ["Pick the right equation of motion", "With constant acceleration: v = u + at, s = ut + ½at², v² = u² + 2as and s = ½(u + v)t. Each one leaves out one quantity: choose the one without the quantity you neither know nor want."], ["Change the frame", "Velocities subtract: the velocity of A relative to B is v_A − v_B. A boat’s velocity relative to the water adds to the river’s; rain that falls vertically seems to slant for a moving cyclist. Choosing the frame where the motion is simplest is often the whole trick."], ["Range, height and angles", "On level ground R = v² sin 2θ / g, greatest at 45°, and the angles θ and 90° − θ give the same range. The greatest height is v² sin²θ / (2g). On a slope rising at α the best angle is 45° + α/2."]], "ex": [{"q": "A ball is thrown at 20 m s⁻¹ at 30° above the horizontal from the top of a 15 m cliff. How far from the foot of the cliff does it land? g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "sol": ["Components: vₓ = 20 cos 30° = 17.32 m s⁻¹, v_y = 10 m s⁻¹.", "Vertically, taking up as positive: −15 = 10t − 4.905t². The positive root is t = 3.044 s.", "Horizontally: x = vₓt = 52.7 m."], "a": "52.7 m"}, {"q": "A swimmer who swims at 1.0 m s⁻¹ in still water crosses a river 50 m wide flowing at 0.60 m s⁻¹. To land directly opposite, where must she aim, and how long does it take? What is the quickest crossing, and where does she land then?", "sol": ["To land opposite, her velocity relative to the water must cancel the current: aim upstream at angle α with sin α = 0.60, so α = 36.9°. Her speed across is 0.80 m s⁻¹ and the crossing takes 62.5 s.", "For the quickest crossing aim straight across: 50 s at 1.0 m s⁻¹, drifting 0.60 × 50 = 30 m downstream."], "a": "36.9° upstream and 62.5 s; or 50 s, landing 30 m downstream."}], "pr": [{"t": "mc", "lv": 1, "q": "A stone is dropped down a well and the splash is heard 3.0 s later. Ignoring the time sound takes, about how deep is the well? g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "ch": ["14.7 m", "29.4 m", "44.1 m", "88.3 m", "9.8 m"], "a": 2, "hint": "The stone starts from rest: s = ½gt².", "sol": "s = ½ × 9.81 × 3.0² = 44.1 m. (Sound would take about 0.13 s of the 3 s, so the real well is a little shallower.)"}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A ball is launched at 25 m s⁻¹ and lands at the height it started from, 50 m away. Find the smaller of the two possible launch angles. g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "a": 25.8511, "u": "°", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Use R = v² sin 2θ / g, and remember that two angles give each range.", "sol": "sin 2θ = Rg/v² = 50 × 9.81/625 = 0.7848, so 2θ = 51.70° and θ = 25.85°. The other solution is 90° − θ = 64.15°."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "Rain falls vertically at 8.0 m s⁻¹. A cyclist rides at 6.0 m s⁻¹. At what angle from the vertical should she tilt her umbrella forward?", "a": 36.8699, "u": "°", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Work in the cyclist’s frame: what is the rain’s velocity relative to her?", "sol": "Relative to her the rain moves 6.0 m s⁻¹ backwards and 8.0 m s⁻¹ down, so it comes from ahead at tan⁻¹(6/8) = 36.9° from the vertical."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 3, "q": "A ball is thrown from the bottom of a slope inclined at 20° to the horizontal, at 15 m s⁻¹, aimed to land as far up the slope as possible. How far up the slope, measured along it, does it land? g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "a": 17.0905, "u": "m", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Tilt your axes along and perpendicular to the slope, or use the trajectory equation and maximise.", "sol": "The best launch is 45° + 20°/2 = 55° above the horizontal, and the greatest range up the slope is R = v²/(g(1 + sin α)) = 225/(9.81 × 1.342) = 17.09 m."}], "traps": ["The time of flight is set by the vertical motion alone: the horizontal speed does not change it.", "At the top of the path the velocity is horizontal, not zero, and the acceleration is still g.", "45° gives the greatest range only when launch and landing are at the same height."]});
WL.pcL({"id": "dyn", "area": "me", "title": "Forces, constraints and friction", "hook": ["Masses of 3.0 kg and 2.0 kg hang on either side of a light, frictionless pulley. One student says the tension is the weight of the 3.0 kg mass, 29.4 N; another says it is the weight of the 2.0 kg mass, 19.6 N. Who is right?", "Neither. The masses accelerate at a = g(3 − 2)/(3 + 2) = 1.96 m s⁻², so for the 2.0 kg mass T − 2.0g = 2.0a and T = 23.5 N. The tension must lie between the two weights: less than 3.0g so the heavy mass speeds up downwards, more than 2.0g so the light one speeds up upwards."], "ideas": [["One body, one diagram", "Draw each body on its own with every force that acts on it, and nothing else. Newton’s third-law partners act on different bodies, so they appear on different diagrams. Then write F = ma for each body along convenient axes."], ["Constraints give the missing equations", "An inextensible string makes connected accelerations equal in size. A movable pulley halves the load’s acceleration compared with the free end. Bodies in contact share the acceleration perpendicular to the contact. Count unknowns and equations before you solve."], ["Friction is “up to μN”", "Static friction takes whatever value stops sliding, up to μₛN. Kinetic friction is μₖN and opposes the relative sliding. To decide which, assume no slipping, find the friction needed, and compare it with μₛN."], ["Choose the axes", "On an incline, use axes along and perpendicular to the slope. In circular motion, take the direction towards the centre: the net inward force is mv²/r. A banked bend needs no friction when tan θ = v²/(rg)."]], "ex": [{"q": "A 5.0 kg block on a 30° incline is tied by a string over a pulley at the top to a hanging 4.0 kg mass. The coefficient of kinetic friction on the incline is 0.20. Find the acceleration once the hanging mass is moving down. g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "sol": ["Along the string, the driving force is the hanging weight minus the block’s weight component along the slope and the friction on the block.", "Net force = 4.0g − 5.0g sin 30° − 0.20 × 5.0g cos 30° = 39.24 − 24.53 − 8.50 = 6.22 N.", "Both masses move together, so a = 6.22/9.0 = 0.691 m s⁻²."], "a": "0.691 m s⁻²"}, {"q": "A car rounds a flat bend of radius 50 m where μₛ = 0.80. What is its greatest safe speed? At what speed would a bend of the same radius banked at 15° need no friction at all?", "sol": ["Flat: friction provides the inward force, μmg ≥ mv²/r, so v ≤ √(μgr) = √(0.80 × 9.81 × 50) = 19.8 m s⁻¹.", "Banked and frictionless: N cos θ = mg and N sin θ = mv²/r, so tan θ = v²/(rg) and v = √(rg tan 15°) = 11.5 m s⁻¹."], "a": "19.8 m s⁻¹ flat; 11.5 m s⁻¹ banked."}], "pr": [{"t": "mc", "lv": 1, "q": "A 2.0 kg box rests on a floor with μₛ = 0.50 and μₖ = 0.40. A horizontal push of 8.0 N is applied. What is the friction force on the box? g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "ch": ["0 N", "7.85 N", "8.0 N", "9.81 N", "19.6 N"], "a": 2, "hint": "Could static friction hold the box still?", "sol": "Static friction can reach μₛmg = 9.81 N, more than the 8.0 N push, so the box stays put and friction exactly balances the push: 8.0 N."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "In an Atwood machine with masses of 3.0 kg and 5.0 kg on a light, frictionless pulley, find the tension in the string. g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "a": 36.7875, "u": "N", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Find the acceleration first, then use F = ma for one mass.", "sol": "a = g(5 − 3)/8 = 2.45 m s⁻², and for the 3.0 kg mass T = 3.0(g + a) = 36.79 N, which is 2m₁m₂g/(m₁ + m₂)."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A block slides from rest down a 25° incline and covers 2.0 m in 1.2 s. Find the coefficient of kinetic friction. g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "a": 0.153878, "u": "", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Get the acceleration from s = ½at², then resolve along the slope.", "sol": "a = 2s/t² = 2.778 m s⁻². Along the slope a = g(sin θ − μ cos θ), so μ = (g sin 25° − a)/(g cos 25°) = 0.154."}, {"t": "mc", "lv": 3, "q": "A light string over a fixed pulley holds a 2.0 kg mass on one end. The other end passes under a light movable pulley carrying a 6.0 kg mass and is fixed to the ceiling. Everything is frictionless. What is the acceleration of the 6.0 kg mass? g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "ch": ["1.40 m s⁻² down", "1.96 m s⁻² down", "4.91 m s⁻² down", "2.80 m s⁻² down", "0: it balances"], "a": 0, "hint": "If the 6.0 kg mass moves down by x, how far does the 2.0 kg mass move?", "sol": "The 2.0 kg mass moves twice as far, so a₁ = 2a₂. The movable pulley gives 6g − 2T = 6a₂, and the 2.0 kg mass gives T − 2g = 2a₁ = 4a₂. Eliminating T: a₂ = (6 − 4)g/(6 + 8) = 1.40 m s⁻², downwards since 6 kg is more than twice 2 kg."}], "traps": ["The normal force is not always mg: on an incline it is mg cos θ, and in an accelerating lift it changes.", "Static friction equals μN only when the object is just about to slip.", "Centripetal force is not an extra force: it is the name for the net inward force."]});
WL.pcL({"id": "con", "area": "me", "title": "Energy and momentum together", "hook": ["A 10 g bullet at 300 m s⁻¹ embeds itself in a 2.0 kg block hanging on strings. How high does the block swing? Can energy conservation alone give the answer?", "Not alone: the bullet stopping inside the block is a very inelastic collision, and most of the kinetic energy becomes heat. Momentum is conserved in the brief collision: V = 0.010 × 300/2.010 = 1.49 m s⁻¹. After that, energy is conserved during the swing: h = V²/(2g) = 11.4 cm."], "ideas": [["Momentum: conserved when no outside impulse acts", "Total momentum is conserved when the net external force is zero, or when it acts for so short a time that its impulse is negligible, as in most collisions. Momentum is a vector: conserve each component separately."], ["Energy: conserved when nothing is lost", "Mechanical energy is conserved for smooth surfaces, elastic collisions and conservative forces. Otherwise use the work–energy theorem: the work done by non-conservative forces equals the change in mechanical energy."], ["The centre-of-mass frame", "In the frame moving with the centre of mass the total momentum is zero. Kinetic energy splits as ½Mv_cm² plus the energy of motion relative to the centre of mass. In an elastic collision every velocity simply reverses in that frame; in one dimension, the relative velocity reverses: v₂ − v₁ = −(u₂ − u₁)."], ["What a sticky collision costs", "When two bodies stick, the energy lost is ½μu², where u is their relative speed and μ = m₁m₂/(m₁ + m₂) is the reduced mass. Only the motion of the centre of mass survives."]], "ex": [{"q": "A 2.0 kg ball moving at 3.0 m s⁻¹ hits a 1.0 kg ball at rest, head on and elastically. Find both velocities afterwards.", "sol": ["Momentum: 2.0 × 3.0 = 2.0v₁ + 1.0v₂. Relative velocity reverses: v₂ − v₁ = 3.0.", "Solving: v₁ = 1.0 m s⁻¹ and v₂ = 4.0 m s⁻¹, both forwards.", "Check the energy: 9.0 J before, and ½ × 2.0 × 1.0² + ½ × 1.0 × 4.0² = 9.0 J after."], "a": "1.0 m s⁻¹ and 4.0 m s⁻¹, both forwards."}, {"q": "A 60 kg skater at rest throws a 5.0 kg ball horizontally at 8.0 m s⁻¹. How fast does she recoil, and how much work did she do?", "sol": ["Momentum: 60v = 5.0 × 8.0, so v = 0.667 m s⁻¹.", "Work = kinetic energy created = ½ × 5.0 × 8.0² + ½ × 60 × 0.667² = 160 + 13.3 = 173 J."], "a": "0.667 m s⁻¹; 173 J."}], "pr": [{"t": "mc", "lv": 1, "q": "A 1500 kg car at 20 m s⁻¹ hits a stationary 1000 kg car and the two lock together. What is their speed just after?", "ch": ["8.0 m s⁻¹", "10 m s⁻¹", "12 m s⁻¹", "16 m s⁻¹", "20 m s⁻¹"], "a": 2, "hint": "Conserve momentum, not energy.", "sol": "1500 × 20 = 2500v, so v = 12 m s⁻¹."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A 1500 kg car at 20 m s⁻¹ hits a stationary 1000 kg car and the two lock together. How much kinetic energy is lost? Give it in kJ.", "a": 120.0, "u": "kJ", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Use the reduced mass, or compare the energies before and after.", "sol": "300 kJ before, ½ × 2500 × 12² = 180 kJ after: 120 kJ lost. The reduced mass gives it at once: ½ × 600 × 20² = 120 kJ."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A neutron (mass 1 u) hits a carbon-12 nucleus at rest, head on and elastically. What fraction of its kinetic energy does the neutron keep?", "a": 0.715976, "u": "", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Find the neutron’s velocity after the collision as a fraction of its velocity before.", "sol": "v₁ = u(m₁ − m₂)/(m₁ + m₂) = −11u/13, so the neutron keeps (11/13)² = 0.716 of its energy. That slow loss is why reactors need many collisions in a moderator."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 3, "q": "A 0.50 kg block slides without friction down a curved ramp 0.80 m high. The ramp, of mass 2.0 kg, stands on a smooth floor and is free to slide. At the bottom the block leaves the ramp horizontally. How fast is the block moving relative to the ground? g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "a": 3.54356, "u": "m/s", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Two unknown speeds: horizontal momentum and energy give two equations.", "sol": "Horizontal momentum stays zero: 0.50v = 2.0V. Energy: 0.50 × 9.81 × 0.80 = ½ × 0.50v² + ½ × 2.0V². So v = √(2gh·M/(M + m)) = 3.544 m s⁻¹, less than the 3.96 m s⁻¹ a fixed ramp would give."}], "traps": ["Kinetic energy is not conserved in an inelastic collision, even though momentum is.", "Use energy across a collision only if the collision is elastic.", "In two dimensions, conserve the x and y components of momentum separately."]});
WL.pcL({"id": "rot", "area": "me", "title": "Rotation and rolling", "hook": ["A solid ball and a hollow ball of the same mass and radius roll from rest down the same slope. Which reaches the bottom first?", "The solid ball. Rolling without slipping shares the energy: mgh = ½mv²(1 + I/(mr²)). The solid ball has I/(mr²) = 2/5 and the hollow one 2/3, so the solid ball keeps a larger share as motion down the slope and is faster at every height. Mass and radius cancel."], "ideas": [["The rotational dictionary", "Every linear idea has a rotational twin: x → θ, v → ω, a → α, m → I, F → τ, p → L. So τ = Iα, L = Iω and the kinetic energy is ½Iω². Torque is force times perpendicular distance from the axis."], ["Moments of inertia", "Rod about its centre ML²/12, about an end ML²/3; disc or solid cylinder ½MR²; ring MR²; solid sphere (2/5)MR²; hollow sphere (2/3)MR². The parallel-axis theorem moves the axis: I = I_cm + Md²."], ["Rolling without slipping", "The contact point is momentarily at rest, so v = ωR and a = αR. Static friction does no work at that point. The kinetic energy is ½Mv² + ½I_cm ω², and the acceleration down a slope is g sin θ/(1 + I/(MR²))."], ["Angular momentum", "With no external torque about an axis, angular momentum about it is conserved: a skater pulling in her arms spins faster. For a particle, L = r × p, which is why Kepler’s second law holds."]], "ex": [{"q": "A uniform disc of mass 2.0 kg and radius 0.10 m rolls from rest down a 30° slope without slipping. Find its acceleration and the least coefficient of static friction that allows this. g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "sol": ["a = g sin θ/(1 + ½) = 3.27 m s⁻².", "Friction alone turns the disc: fR = Iα = ½MR²(a/R), so f = ½Ma = 3.27 N.", "N = Mg cos 30° = 16.99 N, so μ ≥ f/N = 0.192, which is tan 30°/3."], "a": "3.27 m s⁻²; μ ≥ 0.192"}, {"q": "A uniform rod 1.2 m long, pivoted at one end, is released from rest horizontal. Find its angular speed as it passes the vertical, and the speed of its free end. g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "sol": ["The centre of mass falls L/2: mgL/2 = ½(mL²/3)ω².", "So ω = √(3g/L) = 4.952 rad s⁻¹, and the end moves at ωL = 5.94 m s⁻¹, faster than an object dropped through 1.2 m."], "a": "4.952 rad s⁻¹; 5.94 m s⁻¹"}], "pr": [{"t": "mc", "lv": 1, "q": "A hoop, a disc and a solid sphere of the same mass and radius are released together on a slope and roll without slipping. In what order do they reach the bottom?", "ch": ["sphere, disc, hoop", "hoop, disc, sphere", "disc, sphere, hoop", "all together", "sphere, hoop, disc"], "a": 0, "hint": "The smaller I/(mr²), the faster.", "sol": "I/(mr²) is 2/5 for the sphere, 1/2 for the disc and 1 for the hoop, so the sphere arrives first and the hoop last."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A 0.20 kg solid sphere rolls without slipping at 3.0 m s⁻¹ onto a ramp and rolls up it. How high does it rise? g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "a": 0.642202, "u": "m", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Count the rotational kinetic energy too.", "sol": "½mv²(1 + 2/5) = mgh, so h = 1.4v²/(2g) = 0.642 m; the mass does not matter."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A playground roundabout, a uniform disc of mass 200 kg and radius 2.0 m, turns at 1.0 rad s⁻¹. A 50 kg child walks from the centre to the rim. Find the new angular speed.", "a": 0.666667, "u": "rad/s", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "No external torque acts about the axis.", "sol": "I goes from ½ × 200 × 2.0² = 400 kg m² to 400 + 50 × 2.0² = 600 kg m², and Iω is conserved: ω = 400/600 = 0.667 rad s⁻¹."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 3, "q": "A bowling ball, a uniform solid sphere, is launched along the lane at 8.0 m s⁻¹ without spin. Kinetic friction acts until it rolls without slipping. What is its speed then?", "a": 5.71429, "u": "m/s", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Friction acts at the contact point: angular momentum about that point is conserved.", "sol": "About the contact point the friction has no torque, so mv₀R = mvR + (2/5)mR²(v/R), giving v = 5v₀/7 = 5.71 m s⁻¹, whatever the coefficient of friction."}], "traps": ["v = ωR holds only for rolling without slipping.", "Take moments about a point where unknown forces act, and they drop out.", "Kinetic energy is ½I_cm ω² + ½Mv_cm², or ½I_P ω² about the instantaneous contact point P: do not mix the two."]});
WL.pcL({"id": "osc", "area": "me", "title": "Oscillations, from springs to small vibrations", "hook": ["A mass on a spring bounces with a period of 1.0 s on Earth. On the Moon, where g is six times smaller, what is its period?", "Still 1.0 s. T = 2π√(m/k) contains no g: gravity only shifts the point the mass oscillates about, not the stiffness. A pendulum is different: its period would grow by √6."], "ideas": [["Spot simple harmonic motion", "A restoring force proportional to the displacement, F = −kx, gives ẍ = −ω²x with ω = √(k/m) and x = A cos(ωt + φ). The greatest speed is Aω and the greatest acceleration Aω². The period does not depend on the amplitude."], ["The small-oscillation recipe", "Find the equilibrium. Expand the force, or the potential, about it to first order: U ≈ U₀ + ½U″(x₀)(x − x₀)², so ω² = U″(x₀)/m. For a pendulum the torque −mgL sin θ ≈ −mgLθ gives ω² = g/L."], ["Combining springs and pendulums", "Springs in series: 1/k = 1/k₁ + 1/k₂. In parallel: k = k₁ + k₂. A rigid body swinging about a pivot (a physical pendulum) has T = 2π√(I/(mgd)), where d is the distance from the pivot to the centre of mass."], ["Energy in SHM", "E = ½kA², shared between kinetic and potential energy. At displacement x the speed is v = ω√(A² − x²)."]], "ex": [{"q": "A uniform rod 0.90 m long swings about one end. Find its period for small swings, and the length of the simple pendulum with the same period. g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "sol": ["I = mL²/3 about the end, and d = L/2.", "T = 2π√(I/(mgd)) = 2π√(2L/(3g)) = 1.554 s.", "The same period as a simple pendulum of length 2L/3 = 0.60 m."], "a": "1.554 s; 0.60 m"}, {"q": "A small bead slides without friction inside a smooth bowl whose bottom is part of a sphere of radius 0.40 m. Find the period of small oscillations. g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "sol": ["The bowl’s normal force acts like the tension in a string of length R: the bead moves on a circle of radius R, so it behaves as a simple pendulum.", "T = 2π√(R/g) = 1.269 s."], "a": "1.269 s"}], "pr": [{"t": "mc", "lv": 1, "q": "A 0.50 kg mass on a spring of stiffness 200 N m⁻¹ is pulled 4.0 cm from equilibrium and released. What is its greatest speed?", "ch": ["0.40 m s⁻¹", "0.80 m s⁻¹", "1.6 m s⁻¹", "8.0 m s⁻¹", "16 m s⁻¹"], "a": 1, "hint": "v_max = Aω.", "sol": "ω = √(200/0.50) = 20 rad s⁻¹, so v_max = 0.040 × 20 = 0.80 m s⁻¹."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "Two springs, of stiffness 300 N m⁻¹ and 600 N m⁻¹, are joined end to end and hold a 2.0 kg mass. Find the period of vertical oscillations.", "a": 0.628319, "u": "s", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Springs in series: the softer combination.", "sol": "1/k = 1/300 + 1/600, so k = 200 N m⁻¹ and T = 2π√(2.0/200) = 0.628 s."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A thin hoop of radius 0.25 m hangs on a nail and swings in its own plane. Find the period of small swings. g = 9.81 m s⁻².", "a": 1.4185, "u": "s", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Use the parallel-axis theorem for the moment of inertia about the nail.", "sol": "I = MR² + MR² = 2MR² and d = R, so T = 2π√(2R/g) = 1.419 s: the same as a simple pendulum as long as the hoop’s diameter."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 3, "q": "A particle of mass 0.10 kg moves in the potential U(x) = U₀[(x/a)⁴ − 2(x/a)²], with U₀ = 2.0 J and a = 0.50 m. Find the angular frequency of small oscillations about the stable equilibrium at x = a.", "a": 25.2982, "u": "rad/s", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "ω² = U″(a)/m.", "sol": "U″(x) = U₀(12x²/a⁴ − 4/a²), so U″(a) = 8U₀/a² = 64 J m⁻². ω = √(64/0.10) = 25.30 rad s⁻¹."}], "traps": ["The pendulum formula needs small angles: at 60° the true period is about 7 % longer.", "ω in rad s⁻¹ is not the frequency f in Hz: ω = 2πf.", "For a spring hanging vertically, measure displacement from the new equilibrium, not from the unstretched length."]});
WL.pcL({"id": "grav", "area": "me", "title": "Gravitation and orbits", "hook": ["Why do astronauts float inside the International Space Station, when gravity there is still about 90 % as strong as on the ground?", "Because the station and everything in it are falling together around the Earth. In free fall nothing presses on the floor, so nothing feels weight. At 400 km, g is g₀(R/(R + h))² ≈ 0.89g₀."], "ideas": [["Newton’s law and the shell theorem", "F = GMm/r². Outside a spherically symmetric body its gravity is that of a point mass at the centre; inside a uniform shell the pull cancels. At a planet’s surface g = GM/R²."], ["Circular orbits", "Gravity supplies the centripetal force: v² = GM/r and T² = 4π²r³/(GM), Kepler’s third law. A geostationary orbit, with T equal to one sidereal day, has radius about 42 200 km."], ["Energy in orbit", "U = −GMm/r. In a circular orbit the kinetic energy is half the size of U, so E = −GMm/(2r). Escape speed is √(2GM/r). For an ellipse of semi-major axis a, E = −GMm/(2a), and the vis-viva equation gives the speed anywhere: v² = GM(2/r − 1/a)."], ["Kepler’s second law is angular momentum", "Gravity points at the centre, so it has no torque about it, and rv⊥ is constant. At the nearest and farthest points, where the velocity is perpendicular to r, r_p v_p = r_a v_a."]], "ex": [{"q": "Find the radius of a geostationary orbit. GM for the Earth is 3.986 × 10¹⁴ m³ s⁻², and a sidereal day is 86 164 s.", "sol": ["r³ = GMT²/(4π²).", "r = 42 164 km from the Earth’s centre, about 35 800 km above the equator."], "a": "42 164 km"}, {"q": "A satellite in a circular orbit of radius 7000 km fires its engine briefly, raising its speed by 5.0 %. How far from the Earth’s centre does the new orbit reach?", "sol": ["Before the burn v² = GM/r. After it, v² = 1.1025GM/r, and vis-viva says this equals GM(2/r − 1/a).", "So 1/a = (2 − 1.1025)/r and a = 7799 km.", "The burn point is the nearest point, so the farthest is 2a − r = 8599 km."], "a": "8599 km"}], "pr": [{"t": "mc", "lv": 1, "q": "Planet X has twice the Earth’s mass and twice its radius. What is the gravitational field strength at its surface? (9.81 N kg⁻¹ on Earth.)", "ch": ["2.45 N kg⁻¹", "4.91 N kg⁻¹", "9.81 N kg⁻¹", "19.6 N kg⁻¹", "39.2 N kg⁻¹"], "a": 1, "hint": "g = GM/R².", "sol": "g scales as M/R² = 2/4 = ½, so 4.91 N kg⁻¹."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "Find the escape speed from the Moon’s surface. M = 7.35 × 10²² kg, R = 1737 km, G = 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻². Give it in km s⁻¹.", "a": 2.37658, "u": "km/s", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "At escape, the kinetic energy just equals GMm/R.", "sol": "v = √(2GM/R) = 2.377 km s⁻¹."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A comet’s orbit takes it from 0.60 AU to 30 AU from the Sun. Find its orbital period in years.", "a": 59.8463, "u": "years", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Kepler’s third law in solar-system units: T in years, a in AU.", "sol": "a = (0.60 + 30)/2 = 15.3 AU, and T = a^{3/2} = 59.85 years."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 3, "q": "A star of mass 2.0 × 10³⁰ kg and a planet of mass 2.0 × 10²⁷ kg orbit their common centre of mass, 7.5 × 10¹⁰ m apart. How fast does the star move around the centre of mass? G = 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².", "a": 42.1658, "u": "m/s", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Both bodies share the angular velocity; ω² = G(M + m)/d³.", "sol": "ω = √(G(M + m)/d³) = 5.63 × 10⁻⁷ rad s⁻¹. The star circles at d·m/(M + m) = 7.49 × 10⁷ m from the centre of mass, so v = 42.17 m s⁻¹. Wobbles of tens of metres per second are how many exoplanets were found."}], "traps": ["g = 9.81 m s⁻² only at the surface: it falls as 1/r² with r from the centre.", "Orbit radius is measured from the planet’s centre, not its surface.", "Moving to a higher circular orbit takes two speed-raising burns, yet the satellite ends up slower."]});
WL.pcL({"id": "thermo", "area": "th", "title": "Gases, heat and cycles", "hook": ["Push a bicycle pump in quickly and its end gets hot. Push it slowly and it hardly warms. Why?", "A quick push is nearly adiabatic: there is no time for heat to leave, so the work done on the air raises its internal energy and its temperature. A slow push is nearly isothermal: heat leaks out about as fast as the work goes in."], "ideas": [["The ideal gas", "pV = nRT = NkT. The mean translational kinetic energy of a molecule is (3/2)kT, so the root-mean-square speed is √(3RT/M), with M the molar mass in kg mol⁻¹. Temperatures in kelvin, always."], ["The first law", "ΔU = Q − W, where W = ∫p dV is the work done by the gas. For an ideal gas U depends on T alone: U = nC_V T, with C_V = (3/2)R for a monatomic gas and (5/2)R for a diatomic one near room temperature. C_p = C_V + R and γ = C_p/C_V."], ["Four standard processes", "Constant volume: W = 0. Constant pressure: W = pΔV. Isothermal: ΔU = 0 and W = nRT ln(V₂/V₁). Adiabatic: Q = 0, pV^γ and TV^{γ−1} stay constant."], ["Cycles and efficiency", "The net work in a cycle is the area enclosed on a pV diagram. Efficiency is W/Q_in, never above the Carnot limit 1 − T_c/T_h. A refrigerator’s coefficient of performance is Q_c/W, at most T_c/(T_h − T_c)."]], "ex": [{"q": "Air (γ = 1.40) at 20 °C is compressed adiabatically to one-tenth of its volume. Find its final temperature.", "sol": ["TV^{γ−1} is constant, so T₂ = T₁(V₁/V₂)^{γ−1} = 293.15 × 10^{0.40}.", "T₂ = 736.4 K, about 463 °C: hot enough to ignite diesel fuel."], "a": "736.4 K"}, {"q": "1.0 mol of a monatomic ideal gas goes round a rectangle on a pV diagram: from (100 kPa, 10 L) to (200 kPa, 10 L), to (200 kPa, 20 L), to (100 kPa, 20 L) and back. Find the net work per cycle and the efficiency.", "sol": ["Net work = area = 100 kPa × 10 L = 1000 J.", "Heat goes in on the two legs where the temperature rises: at constant volume Q = (3/2)VΔp = 1500 J; at constant pressure Q = (5/2)pΔV = 5000 J.", "Efficiency = 1000/6500 = 15.4 %."], "a": "1000 J; 15.4 %"}], "pr": [{"t": "mc", "lv": 1, "q": "A sealed rigid container of gas at 27 °C is heated to 327 °C. By what factor does the pressure rise?", "ch": ["1.1", "2", "4", "12.1", "600"], "a": 1, "hint": "Convert to kelvin.", "sol": "p ∝ T at constant volume: 600 K/300 K = 2."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "Find the root-mean-square speed of nitrogen molecules (molar mass 0.028 kg mol⁻¹) at 300 K. R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.", "a": 516.948, "u": "m/s", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "v_rms = √(3RT/M).", "sol": "v_rms = √(3 × 8.314 × 300/0.028) = 517 m s⁻¹, faster than sound in air."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "2.0 mol of an ideal gas expands isothermally at 350 K from 10 L to 30 L. How much heat does it absorb? R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.", "a": 6393.7, "u": "J", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Isothermal: ΔU = 0.", "sol": "Q = W = nRT ln(V₂/V₁) = 2.0 × 8.314 × 350 × ln 3 = 6394 J."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 3, "q": "A Carnot engine working between 500 K and 300 K drives a Carnot refrigerator that keeps a freezer at 250 K and rejects heat into a 300 K room. For each joule the engine takes from the 500 K source, how many joules does the refrigerator remove from the freezer?", "a": 2.0, "u": "J", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Find the work per joule, then the refrigerator’s coefficient of performance.", "sol": "The engine makes W = 1 − 300/500 = 0.40 J. The refrigerator’s coefficient of performance is 250/(300 − 250) = 5, so it removes 2.0 J."}], "traps": ["Use kelvin in every gas law: 40 °C is not twice as hot as 20 °C.", "W in ΔU = Q − W is the work done by the gas; some books define it the other way.", "Adiabatic is not isothermal: in an adiabatic expansion the gas cools although no heat leaves."]});
WL.pcL({"id": "em", "area": "em", "title": "Circuits, fields and induction", "hook": ["Twelve equal resistors R form the edges of a cube. What is the resistance between two opposite corners?", "By symmetry, the three corners next to the entry are at one potential, and the three next to the exit at another; joining equal-potential points changes nothing. The current splits three ways, then six, then three: R/3 + R/6 + R/3 = 5R/6."], "ideas": [["Kirchhoff, and the shortcuts", "Current is conserved at every junction; the voltages round any loop add to zero. Series resistances add; in parallel the reciprocals add. A real cell is an emf ε in series with an internal resistance r, so its terminal voltage is ε − Ir."], ["Symmetry and equipotentials", "Points at the same potential can be joined, and a resistor between two of them carries no current and can be removed. An infinite ladder looks the same after one more rung: write R = f(R) and solve."], ["Capacitors and time constants", "Q = CV and the stored energy is ½CV². Through a resistor a capacitor charges or discharges with time constant RC: V = V₀e^{−t/RC} on discharge. Charging a capacitor from a battery always turns half the energy the battery supplies into heat."], ["Magnetic forces and induction", "F = qv × B bends a charge into a circle of radius mv/(qB). A conductor moving through a field has emf BLv. Faraday: ε = −dΦ/dt; Lenz: the induced current opposes the change that drives it, which is why a sliding rod needs a steady push."]], "ex": [{"q": "A 12 V battery with internal resistance 0.50 Ω drives a 5.5 Ω lamp in parallel with a 6.0 Ω heater. Find the current from the battery and the terminal voltage.", "sol": ["5.5 Ω in parallel with 6.0 Ω is 2.870 Ω.", "I = 12/(2.870 + 0.50) = 3.561 A.", "Terminal voltage = 12 − 0.50 × 3.561 = 10.22 V."], "a": "3.56 A; 10.2 V"}, {"q": "A proton at 2.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ enters a 0.50 T magnetic field at right angles. Find the radius of its path and the time for one revolution. m = 1.673 × 10⁻²⁷ kg, e = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.", "sol": ["r = mv/(eB) = 4.18 cm.", "T = 2πm/(eB) = 1.31 × 10⁻⁷ s, independent of the speed: the idea behind the cyclotron."], "a": "4.18 cm; 1.31 × 10⁻⁷ s"}], "pr": [{"t": "mc", "lv": 1, "q": "Two 6.0 Ω resistors in parallel are connected in series with a third 6.0 Ω resistor. What is the total resistance?", "ch": ["2.0 Ω", "4.0 Ω", "9.0 Ω", "12 Ω", "18 Ω"], "a": 2, "hint": "Combine the parallel pair first.", "sol": "The pair gives 3.0 Ω; add 6.0 Ω in series: 9.0 Ω."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A 100 μF capacitor charged to 9.0 V discharges through a 2.0 kΩ resistor. How long does its voltage take to fall to 3.0 V?", "a": 0.219722, "u": "s", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "V = V₀e^{−t/RC}.", "sol": "RC = 0.20 s and V falls by a factor of 3, so t = RC ln 3 = 0.220 s."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A metal rod 0.40 m long slides at a steady 3.0 m s⁻¹ along two rails in a 0.25 T field perpendicular to the rails’ plane. The rails are joined by a 0.60 Ω resistor; everything else has negligible resistance. What force keeps the rod moving?", "a": 0.05, "u": "N", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Find the emf, the current, then the magnetic force on the rod.", "sol": "ε = BLv = 0.30 V, I = 0.30/0.60 = 0.50 A, and the magnetic force BIL = 0.25 × 0.50 × 0.40 = 0.050 N opposes the motion, so 0.050 N is needed. That push supplies the 0.15 W the resistor turns into heat."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 3, "q": "Twelve 1.0 Ω resistors form the edges of a cube. Find the resistance between two corners joined by a single edge.", "a": 0.583333, "u": "Ω", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Inject 1 A at one corner and take it out at the other; use the symmetry about the plane between them.", "sol": "Foster’s theorem: in any network of equal resistors R, the effective resistances across all its edges add up to (N − 1)R, where N is the number of nodes. The cube has N = 8 corners and 12 edges, all alike by symmetry, so each edge gives 7R/12 = 0.583 Ω. Kirchhoff’s laws with the cube’s symmetry give the same."}], "traps": ["Ammeters go in series and voltmeters in parallel; ideal ones change nothing.", "The emf equals the terminal voltage only when no current flows.", "Magnetic forces do no work: they turn a velocity without changing the speed."]});
WL.pcL({"id": "wave", "area": "wo", "title": "Waves, light and modern physics", "hook": ["Why does a soap film look black just before it bursts?", "Light reflected from the front surface is inverted, because it reflects from a medium of higher refractive index; light reflected from the back surface is not. When the film is much thinner than a wavelength the extra path is almost zero, so the two reflections cancel for every colour."], "ideas": [["Superposition and path difference", "Waves add. Constructive interference when the path difference is a whole number of wavelengths, destructive at a half more, after allowing for any half-wave shift on reflection. Double slits give fringes λD/d apart; a grating gives sharp maxima at d sin θ = nλ."], ["Thin films", "At normal incidence the extra path in a film is 2nt. Count the reflections from higher-index media: an antireflection coating of index between air and glass works when t = λ/(4n)."], ["The Doppler effect", "For sound, f′ = f(v + v_obs)/(v − v_src) with speeds counted positive towards each other. Moving the source is not the same as moving the observer. For light at low speeds, Δλ/λ ≈ v/c."], ["Photons and matter waves", "E = hf and p = h/λ; with hc = 1240 eV nm, a 620 nm photon has 2.0 eV. Photoelectric effect: hf = φ + KE_max. Electrons have wavelength λ = h/p. Hydrogen’s levels are E_n = −13.6 eV/n²."], ["Relativity in brief", "γ = 1/√(1 − v²/c²). Moving clocks run slow by γ and moving lengths contract by γ. Energy and momentum: E = γmc², and E² = (pc)² + (mc²)²."]], "ex": [{"q": "Light of wavelength 600 nm passes through two slits 0.20 mm apart onto a screen 1.5 m away. Find the fringe spacing. What happens if the whole apparatus is under water (n = 1.33)?", "sol": ["Δy = λD/d = 600 × 10⁻⁹ × 1.5/(0.20 × 10⁻³) = 4.5 mm.", "In water the wavelength is λ/n, so the fringes close up to 4.5/1.33 = 3.4 mm."], "a": "4.5 mm; 3.4 mm under water"}, {"q": "Ultraviolet light of wavelength 200 nm falls on a metal with work function 4.5 eV. Find the stopping potential.", "sol": ["Photon energy = 1240/200 = 6.20 eV.", "KE_max = 6.20 − 4.5 = 1.70 eV, so the stopping potential is 1.70 V."], "a": "1.70 V"}], "pr": [{"t": "mc", "lv": 1, "q": "A train’s horn sounds at 400 Hz as the train approaches you at 30 m s⁻¹. The speed of sound is 340 m s⁻¹. What frequency do you hear?", "ch": ["365 Hz", "368 Hz", "400 Hz", "435 Hz", "439 Hz"], "a": 4, "hint": "The source moves, not you.", "sol": "f′ = 400 × 340/(340 − 30) = 439 Hz. (435 Hz is what you would hear if you ran at 30 m s⁻¹ towards a stationary horn.)"}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "A diffraction grating has 500 lines per millimetre. At what angle is the second-order maximum for light of wavelength 550 nm?", "a": 33.367, "u": "°", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "d is the reciprocal of the line density.", "sol": "d = 2.0 μm, so sin θ = 2 × 550 nm/2.0 μm = 0.55 and θ = 33.37°."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 2, "q": "Find the de Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated from rest through 100 V. Give it in nm. m = 9.109 × 10⁻³¹ kg, e = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.", "a": 0.122651, "u": "nm", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "p = √(2meV).", "sol": "p = √(2meV) = 5.402 × 10⁻²⁴ kg m s⁻¹, so λ = h/p = 0.1227 nm: comparable to the spacing of atoms, which is why electrons diffract from crystals."}, {"t": "num", "lv": 3, "q": "Muons are made 15 km above the ground and travel straight down at 0.995c. Their mean lifetime at rest is 2.2 μs. What fraction survive to reach the ground, ignoring every other loss? c = 2.998 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.", "a": 0.101997, "u": "", "tol": 0.02, "hint": "Find the time in the ground frame and the dilated lifetime.", "sol": "γ = 10.01. The trip takes 50.28 μs in the ground frame; the dilated lifetime is 22.03 μs. Fraction = e^{−2.283} = 0.102. Without time dilation it would be e^{−22.9}, about 10⁻¹⁰."}], "traps": ["A half-wave shift happens on reflection from a higher refractive index, not a lower one.", "In the Doppler formula, a moving source and a moving observer give different answers.", "Keep photon arithmetic in electronvolts with hc = 1240 eV nm."]});
